body:
Vue version
3.5.40 (latest at the time of writing — the inline repro pins it; also reproduced on 3.5.13, 3.5.17, 3.5.30 and 3.6.0-rc.2; 3.4.38 — before the nested-async chaining existed — crashes with the same null dereference in the older _setParent provides copy inside vnode.ce)
Link to minimal reproduction
https://play.vuejs.org/#
Steps to reproduce

Open the reproduction link and watch the console: mounted x-inner never appears — the element crashes with an unhandled TypeError: Cannot read properties of null (reading 'provides') and never mounts. (The playground variant inserts the nested markup dynamically after the define() calls; dynamic and parser-created markup crash identically.)
Parser-created (static server-HTML-style) variant — self-contained single file, no build step (save as repro.html, serve statically, open in any browser; Chromium, Firefox and WebKit all reproduce):

<!doctype html>
<meta charset="utf-8" />
<title>nested async custom elements repro</title>
<x-outer>
  <x-middle>
    <div>
      <x-inner>inner light dom</x-inner>
    </div>
  </x-middle>
</x-outer>
<script type="module">
  import {
    defineCustomElement,
    defineAsyncComponent,
    h,
  } from "https://unpkg.com/vue@3.5.40/dist/vue.esm-browser.js";

  // Delays chosen so the middle level settles AFTER its child: outer fast,
  // middle slow, inner fast. This crashes on 100% of loads.
  const delays = { "x-outer": 50, "x-middle": 300, "x-inner": 10 };

  const makeComponent = (name) => ({
    name,
    mounted() {
      console.log(`mounted ${name}`);
    },
    render() {
      return h("div", { style: "display:block;padding:6px;border:1px solid #888" }, [name, h("slot")]);
    },
  });

  const makeLoader = (tag) => () =>
    new Promise((resolve) => setTimeout(() => resolve(makeComponent(tag)), delays[tag]));

  // Define the three tags parent-first.
  for (const tag of ["x-outer", "x-middle", "x-inner"]) {
    customElements.define(tag, defineCustomElement(defineAsyncComponent(makeLoader(tag))));
  }

  window.addEventListener("error", (e) => console.error("UNCAUGHT:", e.message));
  window.addEventListener("unhandledrejection", (e) =>
    console.error("UNHANDLED REJECTION:", e.reason?.message ?? e.reason),
  );
</script>
What is expected?
All three elements mount ancestor-first: mounted x-outer, mounted x-middle, mounted x-inner. The nested-async chaining added in 3.5.0 (37ccb9b, "delay mounting of custom elements with async parent", closing #9341 / #8127 / #9351) exists to guarantee exactly this.
What is actually happening?
mounted x-outer
UNHANDLED REJECTION: Cannot read properties of null (reading 'provides')
mounted x-middle

x-inner crashes in VueElement._mount → _inheritParentContext (runtime-dom — parent._instance.provides is dereferenced guarded only by parent && this._app), never mounts, and is left permanently half-resolved: _resolved: true, _instance: null, shadow root empty. Nothing ever retries it, no error surfaces through app.config.errorHandler, and any provide/inject inheritance it should have received is lost. Its own descendants (at deeper nesting) then crash against it in cascade.
Any additional comments?
Analysis (why depth ≥ 3 is the trigger)
connectedCallback of a child under a pending async parent chains:
this._pendingResolve = parent._pendingResolve.then(() => {
  this._pendingResolve = undefined;
  this._resolveDef();
});

For an unchained parent (depth 2), parent._pendingResolve is the parent's own _resolveDef() flow, which settles after the parent has mounted → 2-level nesting is safe. The variants table below confirms: 2 levels never crash.
For a chained parent (depth ≥ 3), parent._pendingResolve is itself a chain promise — and that promise settles when the parent's _resolveDef() returns (i.e. when the parent's async load starts), not when the parent mounts.

So once the first unchained ancestor mounts, the whole chain unwinds within a few microtasks: every deeper level's loader fires nearly simultaneously and mount order degenerates to network settlement order. Any child whose async definition settles before its parent has mounted hits _inheritParentContext while parent._instance === null and throws. With real-network chunk timings the victim set varies per load, so in an app this presents as a flaky, page-dependent breakage; with the fixed delays above it is 100% deterministic.
Variants checked (each re-run in a real browser):



Variant
Result




3 levels, middle slowest
crashes every load


3 levels, inner slowest
clean (race direction confirmed)


2 levels
clean (chained parent required)


plain <div> between levels
still crashes (structure-independent)


markup inserted dynamically after all define() calls
still crashes


all loaders instant
clean (microtask FIFO protects when nothing is pending)



_inheritParentContext (#13219, ~3.5.14) did not introduce the race — it converted a pre-existing silent misordering into a hard crash. 3.4.38 (no chaining) and 3.5.13 (chaining, pre-#13219) fail with the same null dereference in the older _setParent provides-copy path.
Suggested direction — either (or both):

Chain the child to the parent's actual mount, not to _resolveDef() returning — e.g. resolve the parent's _pendingResolve only after _mount() has run, so the guarantee the chaining was built for ("ancestor mounts before descendant") holds at any depth.
Guard _inheritParentContext against parent._instance === null and defer context inheritance until the parent instance exists, instead of throwing away the child permanently on an unhandled rejection.

Vue version
3.5.40
Steps to reproduce
originally reported in nuxt/nuxt#30782

create an options API component with an async setup(), a computed property that reads shared module-level state, and a watch on that
render it on the server and then mutate the shared state after the render is done
see that the watcher is still active on the server

Reproduction
test('options-api watchers should not be created for async setup components', async () => {
  const open = ref(true)
  const spy = vi.fn()

  const App = defineComponent({
    setup: () => Promise.resolve({}),
    computed: {
      isOpen() {
        return open.value
      },
    },
    watch: {
      isOpen: spy,
    },
    render() {
      return h('div', String(this.isOpen))
    },
  })

  const html = await renderToString(createSSRApp(App))
  expect(html).toBe('<div>true</div>')

  // mutating the state after the request is done should not trigger the watcher
  open.value = false
  await nextTick()
  expect(spy).not.toHaveBeenCalled()
})
What is expected?
the same behavior as with a synchronous setup: regular watchers declared via the watch option don't run during SSR and don't survive the render
mutating the shared state after renderToString resolves should not trigger anything
What is actually happening?
when setup() returns a Promise, the watch option creates a live watcher on the server that is never stopped
if this subscribes to state that outlives the request, it keeps the entire component instance (and app) reachable forever
